; Singularity VR mod - settings
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; HookUserPointerDraws
;
; UE3 submits dynamic geometry (particles, decals, sprites) through
; DrawPrimitiveUP / DrawIndexedPrimitiveUP. Those draws are not split per eye,
; so a centred one lands on the seam between the eye halves and disappears -
; the suspected cause of the chest and similar objects vanishing.
;
; Hooking them stopped the game reaching its menus, and a bisection found that
; level 1 (patch installed, forward immediately) works while level 2 (identical
; plus a single counter increment) does not.
;
; ---- run 27: the cause was found, and it was not threading ----
;
; The per-second perf log line had nine printf conversions and TEN arguments -
; the user-pointer counter was missing its %d, so %s formatted an int as a
; char*. That is harmless while the counter is zero and an access violation the
; moment it is not, which is exactly at level 2. The fault happened inside
; Log(), inside a held critical section, so the lock was never released and
; every later log call blocked - a hang, not a crash.
;
; The evidence had been in every log since run 23: "mono-fallback 0(null)".
;
; So level 3 should now simply work. The thread guard is kept regardless -
; issuing device state changes from a thread that does not own the device is
; wrong either way - and the log now reports whether these calls really do
; arrive off the render thread, which was only ever inferred.
;
;   0 = not hooked
;   1 = hooked, forwards immediately
;   2 = level 1 plus a counter        <- this is the one that used to hang
;   3 = full per-eye treatment        <- try this
;
; If it still hangs, set back to 0 and relaunch. No rebuild needed.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

[Render]

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The cold start is flat, as always - no head tracking, no 6-DOF, mono. Press
; F9, F10 and F1 individually as before, OR press BACKSPACE once for all three
; at once (and again to drop back to flat). BACKSPACE is just a shortcut for
; the same three keys; it changes nothing about how the game looks on launch.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; D3D9ExMode   -- the zero-copy prerequisite (run 38). OFF until proven.
;
; OpenXR has no D3D9 binding, so every frame must reach D3D11. Doing that
; without the CPU round-trip needs a SHARED SURFACE, which needs a D3D9Ex
; device, and D3D9Ex rejects D3DPOOL_MANAGED - which UE3 uses for essentially
; everything (spike 2: 10,454 MANAGED allocations against 47 DEFAULT).
;
; So this upgrades the device to Ex and translates the pool:
;
;   vertex + index buffers (7,149)   pool swap only. D3D9 allows Lock() on a
;                                    DEFAULT buffer - it stalls, but these are
;                                    locked once at load, not per frame.
;   textures/cube/volume (3,305)     pool swap PLUS a SYSTEMMEM shadow, because
;                                    LockRect on a DEFAULT texture genuinely
;                                    fails. The game is handed the DEFAULT
;                                    texture and its Lock/Unlock are redirected
;                                    to the shadow, which is uploaded with
;                                    UpdateTexture when unlocked after a write.
;
; What makes this safe rather than merely possible: D3D9Ex does NOT lose
; D3DPOOL_DEFAULT resources on Reset. On a plain device this translation would
; mean rebuilding every resource on every device-lost event.
;
; THIS IS STAGE 1. It changes only how resources are allocated - the frame is
; still delivered by the old CPU copy, so if it works the game should look and
; perform EXACTLY as before. That is the point: it isolates "does the wrapper
; work" from "does zero-copy work". Stage 2 switches the frame path.
;
; What to check in the log:
;
;   D3D9Ex pool translation: 3276 textures, 26 cube, 3 volume, 6258 vertex buf,
;     891 index buf | shadows 3305 (~NNN MB), shadow failures 0,
;     create failures 0, GetSurfaceLevel-on-shadowed 0
;
; Counts should land near spike 2's census. Any "failures" above zero, or
; GetSurfaceLevel-on-shadowed climbing, names the problem directly - the last
; one is the known gap: the engine locking a surface instead of the texture
; would bypass the shadow entirely.
;
;   0 = plain D3D9, MANAGED untouched - the known-good path       <- default
;   1 = D3D9Ex + pool translation
;
; If the game fails to start, set this back to 0. No rebuild needed.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
D3D9ExMode=0

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; ZeroCopy   -- stage 2: what the D3D9Ex upgrade was FOR (run 39)
;
; Only takes effect when D3D9ExMode=1 - a shared surface cannot be created on a
; plain D3D9 device - so leaving this at 1 changes nothing on its own.
;
;   before   GetRenderTargetData (GPU->sysmem, blocks) + LockRect (DMA lands)
;            + memcpy 35 MB + Map + CopyResource        ~9.8 ms at 4096x2160
;   after    StretchRect backbuffer -> shared RT (GPU->GPU)
;            + CopyResource shared -> XR swapchain      target: under 1 ms
;
; The frame never touches the CPU. Spike 1 proved every link in a 32-bit
; process: a D3D9Ex texture with a shared handle, opened by D3D11's
; OpenSharedResource, pixels intact.
;
; Synchronisation is the only genuinely new problem. D3D9 has no counterpart to
; D3D11's keyed mutex, so nothing makes the D3D11 read wait for the D3D9 write.
; A D3DQUERYTYPE_EVENT fence issued AFTER the StretchRect and waited on before
; the D3D11 copy is what spike 1 used and what is used here. That wait is not
; new overhead - run 35 measured it at 2-4 ms of genuine rendering, and a frame
; cannot be shown before it is drawn. What disappears is the ~9.4 ms of
; transfer and memcpy piled on top.
;
; The A/B is D3D9ExMode=1 with ZeroCopy=0 against ZeroCopy=1.
;
; Note the cull-band diagnostic goes inert under zero-copy - there are no
; CPU-readable pixels any more. Set ZeroCopy=0 if you need to measure it.
;
;   1 = shared surface, GPU->GPU                                  <- default
;   0 = CPU round-trip through system memory
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ZeroCopy=1

HookUserPointerDraws=0

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; OcclusionQueryMode   -- THIS IS THE FIX FOR THE VANISHING OBJECTS (run 30)
;
; UE3 draws a bounding box per primitive inside an occlusion query, reads the
; pixel count back, and skips drawing that primitive later if it came back
; zero. In true stereo that readback is wrong, and it was wrong on a huge
; scale: with the results forced to "visible", draws per frame went from ~1000
; to 2100-3800 and every reported object came back solid. The engine was
; throwing away 50-70% of its own draw calls.
;
; "Visible" is conservative: it can never wrongly delete geometry, it can only
; fail to save work. It is however BRUTE FORCE - a proper fix exists and is
; written up in STATUS.md. Do not read this as the final design.
;
; The cost is small and measured, for now. Frame rate held at 37-40 fps with
; peaks of 70, unchanged, because this build is bottlenecked on the CPU frame
; copy rather than on draw calls. Once the D3D9Ex zero-copy path removes that
; bottleneck, ~7,600 real draw calls a frame will matter and this should be
; revisited.
;
;   0 = auto: report visible while true stereo is on, normal in mono  <- default
;   1 = always report visible
;   2 = never override; the engine's own occlusion culling stays live
;   3 = refuse to create them                   ** CRASHES THIS BUILD **
;
; Mode 3 returns D3DERR_NOTAVAILABLE, the documented answer for an unsupported
; query type and the path UE3 takes on hardware without the feature. Run 30
; tried it and the game died between the splash screens and the main menu -
; consistent with the unchecked-NULL-from-a-factory habit already recorded for
; Raven's code. Kept only so nobody retries it.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
OcclusionQueryMode=0

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; FrameCopyMode   -- the performance measurement (run 31)
;
; Getting the game's frame from D3D9 into D3D11 is a CPU round-trip, and it has
; been called this project's bottleneck since spike 2 without ever being
; broken down. It is three costs, not one:
;
;   readback      GetRenderTargetData, GPU -> system memory
;   lock/memcpy   system memory -> the D3D11 upload texture, ~14 MB at 1440p
;   upload        CPU -> GPU again, into the OpenXR swapchain image
;
; Only the FIRST is what D3D9Ex + a D3DPOOL_MANAGED wrapper would remove, and
; that wrapper is a large job - spike 2 measured 10,454 MANAGED allocations in
; one session, every one needing a backing store we own. So it is worth knowing
; which of the three actually costs before writing it.
;
; The per-second "frame copy" log line now reports all three plus the residual
; (frame time minus the copy). Read it before touching anything else.
;
;   0 = pipelined: issue this frame's readback, consume the one from last
;       frame. Costs one frame of latency. MEASURED SLOWER - see below.
;   1 = immediate: capture and consume in the same Present.        <- default
;
; ---- run 32 measured this, and pipelining lost ----
;
;     draws   pipelined            immediate
;       367   107.0 fps (9.34ms)   109.7 fps (9.12ms)
;       444   111.0 fps (9.01ms)   111.3 fps (8.98ms)
;       138   114.5 fps (8.74ms)   117.5 fps (8.51ms)
;
; Immediate is equal or better every time AND has no added latency, so it is
; the default. Mode 0 is kept only so the result can be reproduced.
;
; The interesting part is WHY. Pipelining did not remove the readback stall,
; it relocated it - pipelined reports copy 0.89ms + "our work" 9.4ms, immediate
; reports copy 4.2ms + "our work" 3.7ms, same total. Issuing
; GetRenderTargetData without locking its result lets the call return at once,
; so the phase timer sees nothing, but the driver still owes the transfer and
; it surfaces later inside work that is not broken out. Moving a stall out of
; an instrument's view is not an optimisation.
;
; The latency in mode 0 is handled honestly: each captured frame carries the
; eye, head pose and projection it was actually drawn with, and those are what
; get submitted. Do not "simplify" that by reading the live pose at submit
; time - handing the compositor a fresh pose for a stale image makes
; reprojection correct towards a position the frame never rendered from, which
; reads as swim rather than as lag.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
FrameCopyMode=1

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; GpuFenceProbe   -- does the MANAGED wrapper actually buy anything? (run 34)
;
; The "copy" figure is NOT all round-trip. GetRenderTargetData does not just
; transfer, it SYNCHRONISES - it cannot return until the GPU has finished
; rendering the frame. So the number is really:
;
;     (wait for the GPU to finish)  +  (the actual GPU->CPU transfer)
;
; Only the second is what a zero-copy D3D9Ex path removes. The first is real
; rendering work that would still have to be waited for, just inside the
; compositor instead of inside us.
;
; This matters because the entire case for the D3DPOOL_MANAGED wrapper - weeks
; of work, 10,454 allocations, failure mode "the game will not start" - rests
; on which of the two the ~12.5 ms at 4096x2160 actually is. They cannot be
; told apart from a single merged number.
;
; With this on, an EVENT fence is issued and waited on BEFORE the readback, so
; the sync is paid somewhere it can be timed. The log then reports:
;
;   round-trip split: gpu wait 8.10 ms (rendering - NOT recoverable)
;     | transfer 2.90 + memcpy 2.30 + upload 0.00 = 5.20 ms RECOVERABLE
;     -> zero-copy ceiling 10.80 ms/frame (93 fps)
;
; RECOVERABLE is a CEILING on what the wrapper can buy, not a promise - the
; compositor still has to consume the shared surface.
;
; This adds no work; GetRenderTargetData was going to wait for the GPU anyway.
; It only moves the wait where it can be seen. If total frame time changes when
; you toggle this, the probe is lying - and that is exactly the failure that
; caught out FrameCopyMode, where a stall left the instrument without ever
; leaving the frame.
;
;   1 = split gpu wait from transfer                               <- default
;   0 = off, copy stays one merged number
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
GpuFenceProbe=1

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; 🚨 BEFORE TRUSTING ANY PERFORMANCE NUMBER: TURN OFF SSW
;
; Virtual Desktop's Synchronous Spacewarp (and Meta's ASW) pins the application
; at HALF the headset refresh rate on purpose and synthesises the frames in
; between. Nothing in the log looks wrong when it is on - the timings are
; honest, the frame itself is synthetic.
;
; It cost this project thirty runs. Every frame-rate figure recorded before run
; 32 - "~37 fps at 1440p", the bimodal distribution, the CPU-bound-stall
; diagnosis, and run 31's decision to cancel the D3D9Ex work - was measured
; through that halving. With SSW off at 120 Hz the same build runs at 105-118
; fps and there was no performance problem at all.
;
; The build now logs the display period on the first frame and warns when the
; frame time sits at ~2x it. Check for that line before reading anything else:
;
;   headset display period 8.33 ms (120.0 Hz)
;   *** frame time is 2.00x the display period. SUSPECT SSW/ASW ... ***
;
; Record the refresh rate and the SSW setting next to any number worth keeping.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; There is deliberately no resolution setting here.
;
; Forcing the D3D9 backbuffer does NOT raise UE3's render resolution - the
; engine sizes its scene targets from its own configured resolution, so
; overriding the backbuffer only desynchronises the two and switches stereo off.
;
; Pass it on the command line instead, which the engine reads into its own
; system settings:
;
;   Singularity.exe -ResX=3840 -ResY=2160 -windowed
;
; -windowed is required because fullscreen clamps to real display modes.
; 4992x2688 is refused; the cap appears to be near 4096.
